5 Conclusion
We present iFairy, the first 2-bit complex LLM with all parameters in {±1, ±i}. We integrate complex-valued representations into the Transformer and quantize weights to the fourth roots of unity {±1, ±i} via the proposed PhaseQuant, iFairy fully exploits the 2-bit space while preserving symmetry, efficiency, and hardware compatibility. Experimental results demonstrate that iFairy outperforms the accuracy ceiling of all existing quantization approaches under equivalent model sizes, in terms of perplexity and task accuracy.
Limitations and Future Work. Several limitations remain. First, the optimal formulation of a complex-valued attention mechanism for language modeling is still underexplored. Second, our use of separate scaling factors for the real and imaginary components may not fully preserve the original magnitude structure of complex weights. Third, deploying iFairy in practical systems demands careful hardware-aware design, as current CPU and GPU architectures are not optimized for complex-valued or multiplication-free computation. Future work will focus on scaling iFairy to larger model sizes, exploring unified or learned scaling strategies, and developing hardware accelerators tailored to complex-valued arithmetic. We also envision the design of more expressive, complex-native architectures that further enhance the benefits of complex quantization.


